WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:08.850
- So I'm gonna begin with an example.

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:12.750
So this is a very simple causal model.

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:15.930
It says that the amount
of wheat grown in a field

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:17.970
depends on the amount of rainfall

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:19.520
and the acres of wheat planted.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:25.080
In this model, rainfall and
acreage are exogenous variables.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:29.100
So for example, wheat
growth depends on rainfall,

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:32.130
but rainfall does not
depend on wheat growth.

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:34.980
And if you've done anything
in the social sciences,

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:38.190
you might know that typically
finding an exogenous variable

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:39.600
is a total nightmare.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:41.220
You think something's
exogenous, but it's not.

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:44.340
But this is about as
uncontroversial an example

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:46.740
of an exogenous variable
you're ever gonna find.

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:50.790
But turns out that if we think of these,

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:52.710
not as just single events,

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:54.750
but as things that we're
measuring repeatedly,

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:58.413
so rainfall year after year,
wheat growth year after year,

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:02.103
and if we think about a
long enough timescale,

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:06.390
it could be that over a
hundred years, 200 years,

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:08.700
agriculture does influence climate.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:10.110
At that timescale,

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:12.990
any influence of wheat growth on rainfall

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:16.593
is so slow and so small
that we can just ignore it.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:21.000
Okay, that's just the starting example

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.948
and I'm using it as a way
to illustrate the notion

00:01:23.948 --> 00:01:28.260
of the timescale relativity
of causal representations.

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:30.710
And that's something I'll
be talking a lot about.

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:36.960
Now, in addition to
illustrating that concept,

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:39.240
hopefully this will start to
illustrate the broader theme

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:41.460
of the talk, which is
that the relationship

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:44.400
between time and causation
and causal models

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:46.953
is a lot more intricate
than you might've thought.

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:49.590
So as I'll talk about

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:52.530
when we're talking about graphical
causal inference methods,

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:56.550
so people like Pearl,
Spirtes, Glymour, Scheines,

00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:58.857
yeah, Jenann was mentioning them,

00:01:58.857 --> 00:02:01.230
that these methods do not typically

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:03.420
in fair causal order from time ordering.

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:06.930
So that causes precede their effects plays

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:09.600
surprisingly little role in these methods.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:10.830
But when we look at what's going on,

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:13.380
many of the assumptions
are really assumptions

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:16.317
about temporal stability
and stable behaviors

00:02:16.317 --> 00:02:20.622
arising at particular
spatiotemporal scales.

00:02:20.622 --> 00:02:23.280
And the point of today's talk is to show

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:25.800
how to use dynamic causal models

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:29.340
as developed by Iwasaki and Simon

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:31.917
in order to better
understand these timescale

00:02:31.917 --> 00:02:33.543
and stability assumptions.

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:38.580
Okay, so let me start saying a bit more

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:42.390
about the relationship
between causation and time

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:44.310
in causal models.

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:45.143
What?

00:02:46.251 --> 00:02:49.323
Yeah, so the reference is In
Search of Lost Time Ordering.

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:54.430
Shouldn't be competing with
Proust probably, bad idea, but.

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:58.650
Yeah, so graphical
causal inference methods

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:02.340
do not build time order
into their causal semantics.

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:05.340
So for instance, if we
have these two hypotheses

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:07.410
X causes, Y causes Z,

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:11.070
or X and Z are causes of Y

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:13.830
in standard constraint based methods,

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:15.810
we do not distinguish
between these hypotheses

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:19.380
by saying that if the first is
true, then Y comes before Z.

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:22.410
And if the second is
true Z comes before Y,

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:25.470
but rather in terms of the
probabilistic implications.

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:27.910
So if the first is true, then X and Z

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:31.470
are independent probabilistically
conditional on Y.

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:33.419
If the second is true,
X and Z are independent,

00:03:33.419 --> 00:03:35.583
but dependent conditional on Y.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:41.600
So that is the standard
implications of these models.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:43.650
Of course people will sometimes say

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:46.650
that among observation equivalent models

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:48.573
that you can then use time,

00:03:48.573 --> 00:03:50.623
but it plays often a very secondary role.

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:54.880
And in some ways I think that
this is a really good thing

00:03:56.220 --> 00:04:00.150
for reasons going a
bit back to Tomas talk.

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:01.320
Because on the one hand

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:03.450
I think it's really philosophically useful

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:06.780
to distinguish between time
ordering and causal ordering.

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:08.010
For example, even if you think

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:10.590
that causes always perceive
their effects in time,

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:12.900
you might wanna know why is that the case?

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:14.910
And if you think that it's
just part of the definition

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:17.850
of causation that causes
come before their effects,

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:20.100
then you can't even ask that question.

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:22.320
So I think the fact
that using causal models

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:25.196
you can understand the
probabilistic implications

00:04:25.196 --> 00:04:28.770
of the causal asymmetry
without referring to time

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:32.490
is a huge conceptual improvement.

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:34.410
But that being said,
it's still a bit puzzling

00:04:34.410 --> 00:04:38.130
how small a role time
plays in these models.

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:40.260
And you might actually
just wonder well okay,

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:42.600
like how do we actually
understand the temporal relations

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:44.670
among these variables?

00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:47.073
And I don't think it's
always totally clear.

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:50.139
So for example, when I
give talks to audiences

00:04:50.139 --> 00:04:52.470
and I present directed to ASIC graphs,

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:54.720
so they do not allow cycles

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:57.670
and then someone will
ask oh, is that a problem

00:04:58.535 --> 00:05:00.930
that you don't allow
cycles in these graphs?

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:03.060
And then if I feel just like
giving the standard answer,

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:05.190
I'll say no, no, no, it's
not a problem at all.

00:05:05.190 --> 00:05:08.610
Because even in cases where
you have apparent cycles,

00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:11.010
you can just time-indexing the variables.

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:13.920
So you think there's a cycle
between income and education

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:15.180
'cause people with more income

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:17.520
can get more education and vice versa.

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:19.980
But we just need to talk
about income at time

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:22.110
to equals zero causing education at time

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:24.513
to equals one and so on and so forth.

00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:28.050
And to think to notice about this response

00:05:28.050 --> 00:05:31.350
is it just presupposes that
when we're given a causal graph

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:33.300
and we haven't mentioned
the time relations

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:34.620
among the variables,

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:36.140
they really do have time relations.

00:05:36.140 --> 00:05:37.860
We just haven't said what they are

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:39.460
and we can just time index them.

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:43.620
And I don't think that's the only option.

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:46.710
So again, the time-indexing
option, the idea is just

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:50.100
well, these variables just
implicitly are time-indexed.

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:51.900
They're what philosophers
or meta physicians

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:53.160
would call events.

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:55.980
We just forgot to mention
when they happened,

00:05:55.980 --> 00:05:57.240
even though as Samantha pointed out,

00:05:57.240 --> 00:05:59.440
that would be really,
really useful to know.

00:06:00.706 --> 00:06:03.781
What I suggest instead is
that often what's going on

00:06:03.781 --> 00:06:06.840
isn't that we're just
considering variables

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:08.910
that have times, we
haven't mentioned them,

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:10.470
but rather we're considering variables

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:12.180
that in some sense abstract away

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:15.483
from the temporal relations
among the measured variables.

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:18.690
So here's to give just some
sense of what I mean by that.

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:21.690
Imagine we have some vector variables

00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:23.940
could for example be a time series.

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:25.830
Well, depending on what
assumptions we make,

00:06:25.830 --> 00:06:27.660
we might be able to represent this vector

00:06:27.660 --> 00:06:30.120
of measured variables as a single variable

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:32.291
for the data generating process.

00:06:32.291 --> 00:06:36.720
So for example, if we have
a stationary time series,

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:37.920
then time order doesn't matter.

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:39.660
So we might not need to keep track

00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:42.900
of which variables come
before others in the ordering.

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:44.250
I mean to think of a really simple case,

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:46.230
if all of them were
generated by some variable,

00:06:46.230 --> 00:06:50.940
which was a mean plus a
time stationary error term,

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:52.950
then you could just have a single variable

00:06:52.950 --> 00:06:54.720
for the data generating process.

00:06:54.720 --> 00:06:56.430
Even though you can measure
it at different times.

00:06:56.430 --> 00:06:58.680
'Cause time isn't making a difference.

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:00.300
In the event you have non-stationary,

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:02.105
it becomes more complicated.

00:07:02.105 --> 00:07:04.886
And my point here isn't that
you can't do causal inference

00:07:04.886 --> 00:07:06.487
with non-stationary time series

00:07:06.487 --> 00:07:08.881
just to becomes more complicated.

00:07:08.881 --> 00:07:12.330
And in general I'm just
restraining myself,

00:07:12.330 --> 00:07:14.644
I'd love to talk a lot
more about time series

00:07:14.644 --> 00:07:16.590
and if anyone does wanna talk about it

00:07:16.590 --> 00:07:20.070
either in the Q and A after
the talk, very glad to.

00:07:20.070 --> 00:07:22.260
But the point here is
just to give you a sense

00:07:23.349 --> 00:07:24.630
about what I am suggesting

00:07:24.630 --> 00:07:27.100
when I'm suggesting that we have a model

00:07:27.991 --> 00:07:29.730
where we've just represented some variable

00:07:29.730 --> 00:07:32.620
like pressure or temperature
or height or whatever,

00:07:32.620 --> 00:07:33.810
it doesn't matter

00:07:33.810 --> 00:07:35.970
and we haven't mentioned the time

00:07:35.970 --> 00:07:37.200
and it's because in some sense

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:39.510
we've made some temporal assumption

00:07:39.510 --> 00:07:42.270
that allows us to abstract away
from the temporal relations

00:07:42.270 --> 00:07:44.490
among the measured variables

00:07:44.490 --> 00:07:47.043
and stationary could
be one such assumption.

00:07:49.290 --> 00:07:50.340
Now, more broadly,

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:53.100
I think that there's a historical reason

00:07:53.100 --> 00:07:57.330
for why it is that time is
omitted from causal models

00:07:57.330 --> 00:07:58.163
in this way.

00:07:58.163 --> 00:07:59.610
And I think it has to
do with the development

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:03.180
of causal models as part of econometrics

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:06.058
and as part of the structural
equation tradition.

00:08:06.058 --> 00:08:09.990
And at least historically
the earliest applications

00:08:09.990 --> 00:08:12.930
of these tended to be
systems at equilibrium

00:08:12.930 --> 00:08:16.650
or with relatively stable
time stationary dynamics.

00:08:16.650 --> 00:08:21.030
And I think that history of the
development of causal models

00:08:21.030 --> 00:08:23.330
as part of this tradition
still influences it.

00:08:26.250 --> 00:08:27.720
Now if you accept this story,

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:31.770
I haven't really given
you much evidence for it,

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:34.300
but I'm gonna put it
on the table as a story

00:08:35.176 --> 00:08:36.009
we might think about,

00:08:36.009 --> 00:08:39.120
then the dynamic causal models
that I'm gonna talk about

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:41.880
are gonna be a generalization
of standard causal models.

00:08:41.880 --> 00:08:44.100
'cause while standard causal
models consider a system

00:08:44.100 --> 00:08:46.050
at equilibrium or steady state,

00:08:46.050 --> 00:08:49.470
dynamic causal models generalized
to consider the behaviors

00:08:49.470 --> 00:08:51.963
of the system away from equilibrium.

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:55.049
And the upshot of this philosophically

00:08:55.049 --> 00:08:57.000
is that even though what
I'll be talking about

00:08:57.000 --> 00:08:58.920
in the next few slides
is about the relationship

00:08:58.920 --> 00:09:01.350
between equilibrium and dynamic models

00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:03.570
and that might seem
really, really obscure.

00:09:03.570 --> 00:09:06.990
Like okay, maybe some weird
causal modelers care about it,

00:09:06.990 --> 00:09:11.550
but a serious philosophers
or meta position don't.

00:09:11.550 --> 00:09:14.403
I'm suggesting that even
when people do not explicitly

00:09:14.403 --> 00:09:16.590
talk about equilibrium and dynamics,

00:09:16.590 --> 00:09:18.930
the types of assumptions I'm considering

00:09:18.930 --> 00:09:22.473
are often embedded in the
models and not made explicit.

00:09:26.010 --> 00:09:27.960
Okay, and the last part I'm just gonna,

00:09:29.580 --> 00:09:32.040
yeah I'll skip 'cause
I'll come back to that.

00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:33.420
Okay, and then last preliminary.

00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:34.920
So I'm talking about timescale

00:09:37.100 --> 00:09:38.880
and one way we can
understand is operationally.

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:41.970
So we're sampling some quantity repeatedly

00:09:41.970 --> 00:09:45.150
and we can think about
considering the system

00:09:45.150 --> 00:09:47.650
at a longer timescale as
sampling at a slower rate

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:50.600
to consider it at a shorter timescale

00:09:50.600 --> 00:09:52.920
is to sample it more frequently.

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:55.830
And when I talk about considering
a systematic equilibrium,

00:09:55.830 --> 00:09:58.260
I'm imagining that we're
considering it at timescale,

00:09:58.260 --> 00:10:01.140
which all the variables
have reached steady state

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:03.030
by the time they're sampled.

00:10:03.030 --> 00:10:04.590
And to be totally clear,

00:10:04.590 --> 00:10:06.390
we could talk about
equilibrium and dynamics

00:10:06.390 --> 00:10:09.090
without ever explicitly
talking about timescale,

00:10:09.090 --> 00:10:11.880
but it's helpful in cases
where we wanna model a system

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:14.130
as having a steady long-term equilibrium

00:10:14.130 --> 00:10:16.380
or tending towards a steady
long-term equilibrium

00:10:16.380 --> 00:10:18.300
and then modeling transient dynamics

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:20.550
as perturbations from the equilibrium.

00:10:20.550 --> 00:10:22.110
There's gonna be a relationship

00:10:22.110 --> 00:10:23.730
between the equilibrium dynamic

00:10:23.730 --> 00:10:26.613
and longer shorter timescale distinction.

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:30.941
Okay, so in the next part of the talk

00:10:30.941 --> 00:10:32.820
I'm gonna now just give you the basics

00:10:32.820 --> 00:10:34.680
of dynamic causal models

00:10:34.680 --> 00:10:38.340
and I'm gonna go all the way
back to Herbert Simon 1953

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:40.877
Causal Ordering and Identifiability.

00:10:42.030 --> 00:10:43.740
And if you get nothing
else from this talk,

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:46.050
we should all be reading
a lot more Herbert Simon.

00:10:46.050 --> 00:10:48.432
That's just one of the takeaways.

00:10:48.432 --> 00:10:49.482
It never hurt anyone.

00:10:51.030 --> 00:10:55.020
Okay, so Simon's wondering,
at least in my retelling,

00:10:55.020 --> 00:10:57.750
where does the causal asymmetry come from

00:10:57.750 --> 00:11:00.180
given that many of the basic equations

00:11:00.180 --> 00:11:03.960
of sciences have no built in asymmetry.

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:06.930
So paradigmatically here
we have the ideal gas law

00:11:06.930 --> 00:11:09.150
relating the pressure,
volume, and temperature

00:11:09.150 --> 00:11:11.430
of a gas at equilibrium.

00:11:11.430 --> 00:11:12.420
This is just an equation.

00:11:12.420 --> 00:11:14.343
That's just an ordinary equal sign.

00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:18.090
So this doesn't tell us whether
pressure causes temperature

00:11:18.090 --> 00:11:19.653
or temperature causes pressure.

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:24.330
So where does the causal
asymmetry come from?

00:11:24.330 --> 00:11:28.733
Well Simon says even if we
don't get causal direction

00:11:28.733 --> 00:11:30.420
from this equation alone,

00:11:30.420 --> 00:11:33.480
if we add additional facts
about the system, we do.

00:11:33.480 --> 00:11:36.360
So if for example, there we go,

00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:38.700
we say that it's in a heat bath

00:11:38.700 --> 00:11:40.691
maintaining a constant temperature

00:11:40.691 --> 00:11:43.530
and for example that's
a fixed volume system,

00:11:43.530 --> 00:11:46.230
well now we get the temperature
and volume are exogenous

00:11:46.230 --> 00:11:49.380
and from the values of those variables

00:11:49.380 --> 00:11:51.000
that are exogenously set,

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:54.150
we can determine the value of pressure.

00:11:54.150 --> 00:11:56.430
And the basic idea behind causal ordering

00:11:56.430 --> 00:11:59.550
is that the causal
ordering, what causes what

00:11:59.550 --> 00:12:01.680
corresponds to the order in which we solve

00:12:01.680 --> 00:12:05.580
for the values of
variables in the equations.

00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:06.750
And just to be really clear here,

00:12:06.750 --> 00:12:07.920
there's no magic here.

00:12:07.920 --> 00:12:10.260
This is not telling us how
to get causal conclusions

00:12:10.260 --> 00:12:11.940
without causal assumptions.

00:12:11.940 --> 00:12:13.670
So to say that temperature is exogenous,

00:12:13.670 --> 00:12:16.410
that's a causal assumption,

00:12:16.410 --> 00:12:18.000
but it's just making a bit more explicit

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:21.810
where we get this directionality from.

00:12:21.810 --> 00:12:23.730
And of course we could
have a different setup.

00:12:23.730 --> 00:12:25.830
So it could be the case
that it's immovable piston,

00:12:25.830 --> 00:12:29.670
it's now volume is not fixed,
but equilibrium pressure is,

00:12:29.670 --> 00:12:33.240
and now we're gonna get a
different set of causal relations.

00:12:33.240 --> 00:12:36.390
Importantly, when you can do this solving

00:12:36.390 --> 00:12:38.010
for the causal ordering,

00:12:38.010 --> 00:12:39.330
well then you can rewrite the equations

00:12:39.330 --> 00:12:41.210
as ordinary structural equations.

00:12:41.210 --> 00:12:43.740
So the type of stuff that
we all know and love,

00:12:43.740 --> 00:12:46.380
you can give them a causal
interventionist interpretation,

00:12:46.380 --> 00:12:47.460
you can add error terms,

00:12:47.460 --> 00:12:50.400
then they automatically
satisfy causal mark of,

00:12:50.400 --> 00:12:52.590
so from this basic framework
you can get all the stuff

00:12:52.590 --> 00:12:53.850
that everyone's more familiar with,

00:12:53.850 --> 00:12:55.833
but with an earlier starting point.

00:12:57.420 --> 00:12:59.190
Okay, now why this matters for us

00:12:59.190 --> 00:13:02.490
really has to do this later
paper with Yumi Iwasaki

00:13:02.490 --> 00:13:06.775
where they claim that the
initial causal ordering method

00:13:06.775 --> 00:13:10.260
was initially defined
only for static systems

00:13:10.260 --> 00:13:12.870
consisting of equilibrium equations.

00:13:12.870 --> 00:13:15.930
Now Simon had never really
explicitly said this,

00:13:15.930 --> 00:13:16.770
but certainly you can see

00:13:16.770 --> 00:13:19.080
that in the example I talked about

00:13:19.080 --> 00:13:21.963
we're considering all of these
equations at equilibrium.

00:13:23.580 --> 00:13:27.450
Also historically, Simon was dealing often

00:13:27.450 --> 00:13:31.500
with the simultaneous equations
tradition from econometrics.

00:13:31.500 --> 00:13:32.370
And you might wonder

00:13:32.370 --> 00:13:34.770
well, how can you have
simultaneous equations

00:13:34.770 --> 00:13:37.110
if causation's not simultaneous?

00:13:37.110 --> 00:13:39.420
And the way I'd suggest
we think about them

00:13:39.420 --> 00:13:41.969
is not that there has to
be simultaneous causation,

00:13:41.969 --> 00:13:45.360
just that we're considering
the system at a timescale

00:13:45.360 --> 00:13:47.880
at which the variables
have had sufficient time

00:13:47.880 --> 00:13:50.943
to respond to any perturbations
and reach steady state.

00:13:52.950 --> 00:13:54.510
Okay.

00:13:54.510 --> 00:13:56.790
So now next few slides,
this is the technical part

00:13:56.790 --> 00:14:00.670
of the talk and here I
really just wanna show you

00:14:01.723 --> 00:14:04.803
how these models work and the
basic operations involved.

00:14:06.120 --> 00:14:08.837
Okay, so we just saw an
equilibrium representation

00:14:08.837 --> 00:14:11.160
or in fact two equilibrium representations

00:14:11.160 --> 00:14:15.420
for the you know, two
different ideal gas systems.

00:14:15.420 --> 00:14:19.440
Here I'm just throwing at
you a dynamic causal model.

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:21.570
I'm going to unpack this a bit.

00:14:21.570 --> 00:14:24.925
So basically we're now considering a model

00:14:24.925 --> 00:14:27.660
where we're not assuming that volume

00:14:27.660 --> 00:14:29.400
has had time to reach equilibrium.

00:14:29.400 --> 00:14:33.090
So we're starting by thinking
about movable piston system.

00:14:33.090 --> 00:14:35.340
Here we have a mass on top of the piston,

00:14:35.340 --> 00:14:38.850
which is, there's then a
force on top of the piston,

00:14:38.850 --> 00:14:41.700
which is along with the force
in the bottom of the piston

00:14:42.810 --> 00:14:46.170
influences the acceleration of volume.

00:14:46.170 --> 00:14:49.050
Now these dotted lines,
these are integration links

00:14:49.050 --> 00:14:51.120
and integration links reflect the fact

00:14:51.120 --> 00:14:55.350
that if you know a higher
order derivative of a variable

00:14:55.350 --> 00:14:58.351
then and you know its current value,

00:14:58.351 --> 00:15:02.908
then you can derive its
value at the next time step

00:15:02.908 --> 00:15:05.010
through integration.

00:15:05.010 --> 00:15:07.140
So this is just you know,
integration in the sense

00:15:07.140 --> 00:15:09.810
of the inverse of differentiation.

00:15:09.810 --> 00:15:12.600
So these dotted lines,
the integration lengths,

00:15:12.600 --> 00:15:16.470
they're going to bring
time into the causal model.

00:15:16.470 --> 00:15:17.850
And one thing that's kind of subtle here

00:15:17.850 --> 00:15:20.640
is that because you need to
specify initial conditions

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:22.770
in order to apply integration,

00:15:22.770 --> 00:15:26.490
the initial values of variables

00:15:26.490 --> 00:15:28.170
that are away from equilibrium

00:15:28.170 --> 00:15:30.330
are going to be exogenous.

00:15:30.330 --> 00:15:32.430
So the dynamic causal models will tell us

00:15:32.430 --> 00:15:34.650
how a system evolves
from initial conditions,

00:15:34.650 --> 00:15:37.140
but you're going to need to
specify the initial conditions

00:15:37.140 --> 00:15:38.160
and this is gonna be different

00:15:38.160 --> 00:15:40.473
than just an equilibrium causal model.

00:15:43.050 --> 00:15:45.750
So yeah, and we include time derivatives

00:15:45.750 --> 00:15:47.760
and integration links
only for the variables

00:15:47.760 --> 00:15:51.390
that are considered to
be away from equilibrium.

00:15:51.390 --> 00:15:53.580
Okay, so in any case, we have now volume

00:15:53.580 --> 00:15:54.780
not at equilibrium,

00:15:54.780 --> 00:15:56.580
volume and temperature cause pressure,

00:15:56.580 --> 00:15:58.860
which influenced the
portion in the bottom.

00:15:58.860 --> 00:16:01.320
And just to see how the
equation approach works,

00:16:01.320 --> 00:16:03.000
it works in basically the same way

00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:06.120
as the original causal ordering method,

00:16:06.120 --> 00:16:08.417
except we have some more
exogenous variables.

00:16:08.417 --> 00:16:11.010
'Cause first of all, we've added mass

00:16:11.010 --> 00:16:14.010
and also the initial
conditions are exogenous.

00:16:14.010 --> 00:16:17.370
And then we solve for the
equations in the same way.

00:16:17.370 --> 00:16:20.200
So mass determines the force in the top

00:16:21.077 --> 00:16:26.077
and the ideal gas law gives us pressure

00:16:26.296 --> 00:16:28.110
from volume and temperature

00:16:28.110 --> 00:16:30.120
and then the force in the bottom

00:16:30.120 --> 00:16:32.070
is proportional to the pressure.

00:16:32.070 --> 00:16:33.820
And then using Newton's second law,

00:16:33.820 --> 00:16:38.763
well we combine the forces in
order to get the acceleration.

00:16:40.590 --> 00:16:42.393
Okay, so that's the basic model.

00:16:44.250 --> 00:16:46.020
Often when I show this to scientists,

00:16:46.020 --> 00:16:48.270
at least to scientists
that are willing to listen,

00:16:48.270 --> 00:16:50.190
this is way too much.

00:16:50.190 --> 00:16:53.340
They don't like the cycles,
it's really kind of scary.

00:16:53.340 --> 00:16:55.740
So just if it makes you happy,

00:16:55.740 --> 00:16:58.380
you can also unfold this in time.

00:16:58.380 --> 00:17:00.210
Basically treat all the variables

00:17:00.210 --> 00:17:03.390
connected by ordinary causal
arrows is simultaneous.

00:17:03.390 --> 00:17:08.390
And the integration link says
giving you cross time step

00:17:10.830 --> 00:17:14.801
causal relationships and you
can unpack it in this way.

00:17:14.801 --> 00:17:15.750
And one thing you notice is

00:17:15.750 --> 00:17:18.150
that even though there's a
cycle in the dynamic graph,

00:17:18.150 --> 00:17:21.161
it's not technically cyclic.

00:17:21.161 --> 00:17:23.190
And yeah, so if this graph

00:17:23.190 --> 00:17:25.800
helps you understand things better, great.

00:17:25.800 --> 00:17:28.200
Notice that we're still
not using time ordering

00:17:28.200 --> 00:17:30.090
to solve for causal ordering.

00:17:30.090 --> 00:17:33.000
The really crucial information
contained in this graph

00:17:33.000 --> 00:17:37.050
isn't that you know, we're
looking at what proceeds what

00:17:37.050 --> 00:17:38.880
and using that to solve
for the time ordering.

00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:42.690
What really matters is
that we have these clusters

00:17:42.690 --> 00:17:45.810
of variables influencing one
another at different rates,

00:17:45.810 --> 00:17:47.880
such that clusters of variables

00:17:47.880 --> 00:17:49.410
that are very tightly connected

00:17:49.410 --> 00:17:51.829
and influence one another
quickly at a timescale.

00:17:51.829 --> 00:17:54.180
Those are influences simultaneous

00:17:54.180 --> 00:17:55.800
and influences that take,

00:17:55.800 --> 00:17:58.170
they're treated as taking
time over that timescale

00:17:58.170 --> 00:17:59.703
are treated diachronically.

00:18:00.570 --> 00:18:02.250
So this type of representation

00:18:02.250 --> 00:18:05.910
is capturing timescale separation facts

00:18:05.910 --> 00:18:07.530
as opposed to using the time order

00:18:07.530 --> 00:18:09.785
to solve for the causal ordering.

00:18:09.785 --> 00:18:11.910
Now most importantly for us today

00:18:11.910 --> 00:18:16.006
is when you use the compact
not rolled out representation,

00:18:16.006 --> 00:18:19.890
you can apply an operation
known as equilibration.

00:18:19.890 --> 00:18:22.080
And equilibration is
when you take a variable

00:18:22.080 --> 00:18:25.500
in a dynamic graph that
has not reached equilibrium

00:18:25.500 --> 00:18:27.630
and you apply equilibration

00:18:27.630 --> 00:18:30.393
to determine what the
equilibrium graph will be.

00:18:31.410 --> 00:18:33.090
Okay, so this works as follows.

00:18:33.090 --> 00:18:36.173
So first you set all
derivatives of volume to zero,

00:18:36.173 --> 00:18:38.760
remove them from the model.

00:18:38.760 --> 00:18:42.300
You also delete all
equations going into volume

00:18:42.300 --> 00:18:43.680
and remove the derivatives.

00:18:43.680 --> 00:18:46.060
This includes the integration equations

00:18:47.010 --> 00:18:49.590
and then you remap using
the causal ordering.

00:18:49.590 --> 00:18:52.500
And perhaps unsurprisingly,
when you do that

00:18:52.500 --> 00:18:56.880
you end up with well, one of
our initial causal models,

00:18:56.880 --> 00:18:57.713
namely the one

00:18:57.713 --> 00:18:59.890
in which pressure and
temperature cause volume.

00:19:01.800 --> 00:19:03.350
I'll say a bit more about that.

00:19:04.269 --> 00:19:05.520
But again, the important thing here is

00:19:05.520 --> 00:19:07.050
that equilibration takes a model

00:19:07.050 --> 00:19:12.050
in which you had a feedback
loop and you derive the model

00:19:12.150 --> 00:19:14.640
that would result from
equilibrating that feedback loop.

00:19:14.640 --> 00:19:16.980
So allowing the system
to run to equilibrium

00:19:16.980 --> 00:19:18.633
as a result of self-regulation.

00:19:20.220 --> 00:19:23.580
Okay, much more familiar is
the notion of intervention.

00:19:23.580 --> 00:19:25.830
So that's just the do operator

00:19:25.830 --> 00:19:29.820
and sorry gave away the punchline.

00:19:29.820 --> 00:19:31.530
And the only sort of surprise here is

00:19:31.530 --> 00:19:33.420
that when you intervene on a variable

00:19:33.420 --> 00:19:34.680
with derivatives in the model,

00:19:34.680 --> 00:19:37.140
you also intervene on as
higher order derivatives.

00:19:37.140 --> 00:19:39.750
So the thought is
something like you can't,

00:19:39.750 --> 00:19:42.289
you know, intervene on
the position of an object

00:19:42.289 --> 00:19:46.590
without also setting its velocity
and acceleration to zero.

00:19:46.590 --> 00:19:49.270
Okay, so when we do that,
we get a different model

00:19:51.000 --> 00:19:52.620
and in this particular case

00:19:52.620 --> 00:19:55.650
we saw that with the
dynamic model I gave you,

00:19:55.650 --> 00:19:58.350
if you intervene you get one model,

00:19:58.350 --> 00:20:02.150
the one corresponding to
the fixed volume system.

00:20:02.150 --> 00:20:04.290
If you equilibrate you
get a different model,

00:20:04.290 --> 00:20:07.320
the one correspond to the movable piston.

00:20:07.320 --> 00:20:09.810
And without going too
much into the details,

00:20:09.810 --> 00:20:11.340
the fact you get these two models

00:20:11.340 --> 00:20:14.400
has actually been a source
of lots of consternation.

00:20:14.400 --> 00:20:16.893
So Denver Dash in his dissertation,

00:20:17.760 --> 00:20:19.320
he thought that this was really a problem

00:20:19.320 --> 00:20:21.660
with equilibrium models

00:20:21.660 --> 00:20:25.080
for reasons that I'm not
gonna really go deeply into.

00:20:25.080 --> 00:20:27.767
What I wanna argue is that
actually it's the right result

00:20:27.767 --> 00:20:30.960
that we get these two
different equilibrium models.

00:20:30.960 --> 00:20:32.040
And the reason is that basically

00:20:32.040 --> 00:20:35.070
what equilibration is telling you is

00:20:35.070 --> 00:20:39.000
what happens if you allow the
system to reach equilibrium

00:20:39.000 --> 00:20:42.183
via a feedback loop

00:20:42.183 --> 00:20:45.690
that you know allows it to self-regulate.

00:20:45.690 --> 00:20:48.510
Now when you in contrast
intervene on volume,

00:20:48.510 --> 00:20:52.080
you're not allowing that
feedback loop to operate.

00:20:52.080 --> 00:20:54.120
And as some of you probably know,

00:20:54.120 --> 00:20:55.900
feedback loops are necessary

00:20:56.848 --> 00:20:58.830
to obtain certain types of stability.

00:20:58.830 --> 00:21:00.600
So when you intervene on the system

00:21:00.600 --> 00:21:02.220
and when you intervene
not just temporarily

00:21:02.220 --> 00:21:03.750
but hold volume fixed,

00:21:03.750 --> 00:21:06.150
you're not allowing the
feedback loop to operate,

00:21:06.150 --> 00:21:10.680
you're destroying the
stability producing property

00:21:10.680 --> 00:21:11.513
of the system

00:21:11.513 --> 00:21:13.560
and you're bringing it to
a different stable state,

00:21:13.560 --> 00:21:15.990
the one corresponding to
the fixed volume container.

00:21:15.990 --> 00:21:17.550
So I think what's going on here

00:21:17.550 --> 00:21:19.530
is that we just have these two systems

00:21:19.530 --> 00:21:22.920
that with two relations
and the interesting result

00:21:22.920 --> 00:21:24.780
is that intervening and equilibrating

00:21:24.780 --> 00:21:25.680
just bring the system

00:21:25.680 --> 00:21:28.083
into two different incompatible states.

00:21:30.990 --> 00:21:33.810
Okay, so that was the
main technical parts.

00:21:33.810 --> 00:21:38.190
Now we can sum up how
this relates to timescale.

00:21:38.190 --> 00:21:40.200
So first of all, so we saw equilibration,

00:21:40.200 --> 00:21:43.440
which once again it takes a feedback loop

00:21:43.440 --> 00:21:45.739
that in one model reflects a variable

00:21:45.739 --> 00:21:48.150
that has not reached equilibrium

00:21:48.150 --> 00:21:49.950
and involves treating the reaction time

00:21:49.950 --> 00:21:52.200
as if it were instantaneous.

00:21:52.200 --> 00:21:55.110
Now there's another operation,
which I'm gonna describe

00:21:55.110 --> 00:21:58.361
called exogenization, which
involves treating a variable

00:21:58.361 --> 00:22:02.913
as changing extremely slowly
as if it were constant.

00:22:04.110 --> 00:22:05.850
And using the timescale
picture we could think

00:22:05.850 --> 00:22:07.860
about these operations
in terms of zooming out

00:22:07.860 --> 00:22:09.270
and zooming in.

00:22:09.270 --> 00:22:12.768
So equilibration is when
we zoom out to a timescale

00:22:12.768 --> 00:22:17.100
at which something that
at the previous timescale

00:22:17.100 --> 00:22:18.990
was taken time to occur but we zoom out

00:22:18.990 --> 00:22:21.249
and we could treat it as instantaneous.

00:22:21.249 --> 00:22:24.052
While exogenization is obviously
changing at a timescale

00:22:24.052 --> 00:22:25.910
and we zoom into a timescale

00:22:25.910 --> 00:22:28.140
at which the rate of change is so slow

00:22:28.140 --> 00:22:29.890
that we could treat it as constant.

00:22:31.470 --> 00:22:34.410
Now going back to our original example,

00:22:34.410 --> 00:22:36.412
we can now understand it
in terms of exogenization.

00:22:36.412 --> 00:22:38.040
So once again we had this case

00:22:38.040 --> 00:22:40.860
where we had one model without a cycle

00:22:40.860 --> 00:22:44.730
and where rainfall was exogenous,
another one with a cycle.

00:22:44.730 --> 00:22:46.740
And it's a very simple example,

00:22:46.740 --> 00:22:48.720
but the punchline is that exogenization

00:22:48.720 --> 00:22:52.050
is the operation that
allows one to treat rainfall

00:22:52.050 --> 00:22:53.460
as exogenous.

00:22:53.460 --> 00:22:56.920
And the big takeaway of that for timescale

00:22:58.109 --> 00:23:00.390
is that Iwasaki and Simon's
dynamic causal models

00:23:00.390 --> 00:23:03.050
provide a framework for
capturing this timescale

00:23:03.050 --> 00:23:05.760
of relativity of causal relationships.

00:23:05.760 --> 00:23:09.180
So these operations allow us
to understand the relationships

00:23:09.180 --> 00:23:11.940
between causal models
at different timescales

00:23:11.940 --> 00:23:13.263
in a systematic way.

00:23:14.490 --> 00:23:17.100
And one thing to note is
that the relationships

00:23:17.100 --> 00:23:20.553
can really look very different
at the different timescales.

00:23:21.480 --> 00:23:22.800
Okay, now the rest of the talk,

00:23:22.800 --> 00:23:24.600
I'm gonna quickly do some implications

00:23:24.600 --> 00:23:26.280
'cause really what I
wanna do in this talk,

00:23:26.280 --> 00:23:28.740
I mean again I'm happy to talk
about the technical details

00:23:28.740 --> 00:23:30.690
with anyone who wants to get into them,

00:23:31.902 --> 00:23:34.710
but I really think that
this whole framework

00:23:34.710 --> 00:23:36.120
and this whole way of thinking,

00:23:36.120 --> 00:23:38.190
both in terms of timescale and stability,

00:23:38.190 --> 00:23:41.610
it introduces lots of things
into the philosophical

00:23:41.610 --> 00:23:43.410
and scientific study of causation

00:23:43.410 --> 00:23:44.850
that at least as far as I could tell

00:23:44.850 --> 00:23:46.350
other people aren't talking about.

00:23:46.350 --> 00:23:48.426
And I really just wanna convince you all

00:23:48.426 --> 00:23:51.630
that you should actually
think about it as well.

00:23:51.630 --> 00:23:53.363
Okay, so here are some implications.

00:23:53.363 --> 00:23:55.743
This one, first one is philosophical.

00:23:56.760 --> 00:23:59.437
So as Cartwright told all of us,

00:23:59.437 --> 00:24:01.800
"no causes in, no causes out."

00:24:01.800 --> 00:24:03.550
I agree with Cartwright on this

00:24:04.440 --> 00:24:06.360
and that means, for example,

00:24:06.360 --> 00:24:08.070
you can't define something like exogeneity

00:24:08.070 --> 00:24:10.230
without causal assumptions.

00:24:10.230 --> 00:24:12.723
And I again totally agree with that,

00:24:13.770 --> 00:24:15.390
but I think sometimes people use that

00:24:15.390 --> 00:24:16.890
as a way to end the conversation

00:24:16.890 --> 00:24:17.850
and say like okay,

00:24:17.850 --> 00:24:19.110
well then there's just nothing we can say.

00:24:19.110 --> 00:24:20.370
You just, you know, you say how do I know

00:24:20.370 --> 00:24:21.450
this variable is exogenous?

00:24:21.450 --> 00:24:23.370
And they say like look
at your scientific theory

00:24:23.370 --> 00:24:26.140
or something and you go
well, thanks so much.

00:24:26.140 --> 00:24:27.750
I have no idea.

00:24:27.750 --> 00:24:31.090
And I think that we can say more about it,

00:24:31.090 --> 00:24:34.920
and I think seeing how
assumptions that this exogeneity

00:24:34.920 --> 00:24:37.380
vary with the timescale at
which we consider system

00:24:37.380 --> 00:24:40.620
does tell us something
about how the world is

00:24:40.620 --> 00:24:44.490
and needs to be in order for
us to make these assumptions.

00:24:44.490 --> 00:24:46.650
So we can see for example, that you know,

00:24:46.650 --> 00:24:49.461
in this simple crop example

00:24:49.461 --> 00:24:52.020
that well we're saying it's exogenous

00:24:52.020 --> 00:24:54.060
and we can allow that the
world's more complicated.

00:24:54.060 --> 00:24:56.706
But if we genuinely think the influence

00:24:56.706 --> 00:24:57.993
is minor and slow acting,

00:24:58.842 --> 00:25:01.136
that would be a legitimizing assumption

00:25:01.136 --> 00:25:03.436
for treating it as
exogenous at the timescale.

00:25:04.290 --> 00:25:06.390
Additionally, I think
this does have some payoff

00:25:06.390 --> 00:25:09.030
for some philosophical debates

00:25:09.030 --> 00:25:11.970
including engaging with people like Janan.

00:25:11.970 --> 00:25:15.543
So this, the previous
talk set this up nicely.

00:25:15.543 --> 00:25:18.450
So one thing Janan writes about

00:25:18.450 --> 00:25:22.170
in how causes depend on us is
building on the perian picture

00:25:22.170 --> 00:25:24.036
she referred to, there's this idea

00:25:24.036 --> 00:25:26.640
that causal relationships are relative

00:25:26.640 --> 00:25:30.738
to the choice of exogenous
and endogenous variables.

00:25:30.738 --> 00:25:32.610
And she has her own story

00:25:32.610 --> 00:25:36.120
about how this choice does
depend on features of agency.

00:25:36.120 --> 00:25:40.680
And I'm not gonna really risk
saying anything too ambitious

00:25:40.680 --> 00:25:43.560
in this short talk, but
I'm just making the claim

00:25:43.560 --> 00:25:47.160
that having a more, well,
having a general theory,

00:25:47.160 --> 00:25:48.720
a theory of the type I'm providing

00:25:48.720 --> 00:25:50.640
of what it is in the world that allows you

00:25:50.640 --> 00:25:52.170
to think systematically about exogeneity

00:25:52.170 --> 00:25:55.680
will also allow us to
continue that discussion.

00:25:55.680 --> 00:25:57.570
And similarly for
discussions that have come up

00:25:57.570 --> 00:26:00.210
in several of the talks
about is there causation

00:26:00.210 --> 00:26:02.587
in closed deterministic systems

00:26:02.587 --> 00:26:03.990
where you sort of reach a standoff

00:26:03.990 --> 00:26:06.780
because again, going back to Janan's talk,

00:26:06.780 --> 00:26:08.187
we understand in open systems,

00:26:08.187 --> 00:26:10.020
what the exogenous factors are,

00:26:10.020 --> 00:26:11.880
but let's just kind of generalize out

00:26:11.880 --> 00:26:14.032
and think about the universe of the whole.

00:26:14.032 --> 00:26:17.016
And we can define exogenous
risk of variation.

00:26:17.016 --> 00:26:20.670
We don't know what they
refer to in the world.

00:26:20.670 --> 00:26:24.180
And again, I just think
having more resources

00:26:24.180 --> 00:26:26.610
to not just stipulate that
certain things are exogenous

00:26:26.610 --> 00:26:28.830
but also think about
in cases we understand

00:26:28.830 --> 00:26:29.970
what are the features of the world

00:26:29.970 --> 00:26:31.650
that allow us to treat it like such.

00:26:31.650 --> 00:26:34.293
Seems to me like a way to
advance this discussion.

00:26:35.439 --> 00:26:38.700
Something else, this is
more on the modeling side.

00:26:38.700 --> 00:26:40.560
So there's lots of debates about causation

00:26:40.560 --> 00:26:42.857
in complex systems and here

00:26:42.857 --> 00:26:45.512
I'm just gonna quickly illustrate

00:26:45.512 --> 00:26:47.430
again, without giving the details

00:26:47.430 --> 00:26:52.430
of how we can use these types of models

00:26:52.470 --> 00:26:55.620
to really think about some
standard complex systems.

00:26:55.620 --> 00:26:57.375
So one that gets talked about a lot,

00:26:57.375 --> 00:27:01.230
especially in cognitive
science, is the watt governor.

00:27:01.230 --> 00:27:02.880
So this is a device for
regulating the speed

00:27:02.880 --> 00:27:04.380
of steam engines.

00:27:04.380 --> 00:27:06.570
The idea was is that people
were using steam engines

00:27:06.570 --> 00:27:11.550
to do work, say hooking them
up to a loom for weaving.

00:27:11.550 --> 00:27:13.350
You want them to do regular work,

00:27:13.350 --> 00:27:15.855
but the work sources are not constant.

00:27:15.855 --> 00:27:17.790
There could be changes in the workload,

00:27:17.790 --> 00:27:20.250
changes in the you know, coal.

00:27:20.250 --> 00:27:23.430
So this was a device
that instead of having

00:27:23.430 --> 00:27:26.010
to have some human there, as
you might think you would,

00:27:26.010 --> 00:27:28.908
see the changes in workload
and respond to them,

00:27:28.908 --> 00:27:32.760
this is a control feedback loop

00:27:32.760 --> 00:27:37.590
where what happens is
you have these fly balls,

00:27:37.590 --> 00:27:41.100
which have a mass that
are connected to a spindle

00:27:41.100 --> 00:27:43.350
and if for example,

00:27:43.350 --> 00:27:47.820
oh and then the spindle
is rotating proportional

00:27:47.820 --> 00:27:51.030
to the speed of the steam engine engine.

00:27:51.030 --> 00:27:54.330
If, for example, the steam
engine starts to speed up

00:27:54.330 --> 00:27:56.400
above the desired value,

00:27:56.400 --> 00:28:01.400
the fly balls start to go out
due to the centrifugal force

00:28:01.440 --> 00:28:04.350
and then the spindles are hooked up

00:28:04.350 --> 00:28:09.350
to the throttle valve in a way
that as the flyballs go up,

00:28:10.705 --> 00:28:12.780
the valve is slightly shut.

00:28:12.780 --> 00:28:14.400
So you get a negative feedback loop,

00:28:14.400 --> 00:28:16.740
increases in speed above the desired value

00:28:16.740 --> 00:28:19.320
cause the system to
automatically slow down,

00:28:19.320 --> 00:28:23.373
let less steam into the
engine and vice versa.

00:28:24.660 --> 00:28:26.610
So this is again widely discussed

00:28:26.610 --> 00:28:28.290
in cognitive science especially.

00:28:28.290 --> 00:28:31.166
It's also just a kind of paradigm example

00:28:31.166 --> 00:28:33.485
and at least mathematical discussions

00:28:33.485 --> 00:28:37.173
of dynamical systems and control systems.

00:28:38.190 --> 00:28:40.500
In reality, people don't use
this design so much anymore,

00:28:40.500 --> 00:28:41.790
but that's a, you know,

00:28:41.790 --> 00:28:44.694
but it's a nice mathematical model.

00:28:44.694 --> 00:28:46.830
So if you're ever in Munich
you can also see lots of them

00:28:46.830 --> 00:28:48.063
at the Deutsches Museum.

00:28:51.090 --> 00:28:55.770
So in Van Gelder's
seminal discussion of this

00:28:55.770 --> 00:28:58.320
as a sort of model of dynamical cognition,

00:28:58.320 --> 00:29:02.550
he makes the claim that when
we understand the behavior,

00:29:02.550 --> 00:29:05.176
the Watts governor, the
system's entire operation

00:29:05.176 --> 00:29:06.900
is smooth and continuous.

00:29:06.900 --> 00:29:09.330
There's no possibility of
non arbitrarily dividing

00:29:09.330 --> 00:29:12.480
of these changes over time
and the distinct manipulations

00:29:12.480 --> 00:29:14.193
and no point in trying to do so.

00:29:15.390 --> 00:29:19.200
So here he's giving an
argument against why,

00:29:19.200 --> 00:29:20.940
what this system is doing
shouldn't be understood

00:29:20.940 --> 00:29:22.533
using a computational model.

00:29:24.150 --> 00:29:26.040
That's obviously not what what
we're talking about today.

00:29:26.040 --> 00:29:27.630
But I think this type of argument

00:29:27.630 --> 00:29:30.570
or this type of perception
of complex dynamical systems

00:29:30.570 --> 00:29:32.277
as being such that every part

00:29:32.277 --> 00:29:36.330
influences every other part
at the same rate irreducibly

00:29:36.330 --> 00:29:39.090
also is the type of argument
you see people giving

00:29:39.090 --> 00:29:42.250
against causal interpretations
of dynamical systems

00:29:43.140 --> 00:29:45.330
and therefore it's somewhat significant

00:29:45.330 --> 00:29:48.630
that when you actually try to
model the system dynamically

00:29:48.630 --> 00:29:51.060
and causally you see that in fact

00:29:51.060 --> 00:29:53.734
it is not such that
you can't decompose it.

00:29:53.734 --> 00:29:56.733
And in fact you can give a causal model

00:29:56.733 --> 00:29:59.169
of the sort I've been giving.

00:29:59.169 --> 00:30:01.953
So basically, and without
getting into the details,

00:30:03.390 --> 00:30:06.000
you have this causal
model showing how theta,

00:30:06.000 --> 00:30:08.220
the angle of the arms
depends on gravity length

00:30:08.220 --> 00:30:09.810
and the speed of the engine

00:30:09.810 --> 00:30:14.040
and how the angle of the
arms determines the steam

00:30:14.040 --> 00:30:15.900
led into the engine.

00:30:15.900 --> 00:30:20.013
P is the workload and those
determine the change in speed.

00:30:20.013 --> 00:30:23.610
So this is just an example of how to use

00:30:23.610 --> 00:30:25.432
these types of models

00:30:25.432 --> 00:30:28.200
to model a slightly more
complicated dynamical system

00:30:28.200 --> 00:30:33.200
and hopefully give you some confidence

00:30:33.330 --> 00:30:34.320
that we can generalize this

00:30:34.320 --> 00:30:36.484
'cause these types of
feedback loops are not rare.

00:30:36.484 --> 00:30:39.570
So this system is still relatively simple,

00:30:39.570 --> 00:30:41.667
but the use of control
feedback loops is not,

00:30:41.667 --> 00:30:45.660
and here we see that you are
able to model it causally.

00:30:45.660 --> 00:30:48.180
And as with the previous part of the doc,

00:30:48.180 --> 00:30:50.880
I'm not make gonna make
some huge sweeping claim

00:30:50.880 --> 00:30:53.340
about you know what this
tells us about causation

00:30:53.340 --> 00:30:54.990
and dynamical systems.

00:30:54.990 --> 00:30:57.510
I'll just say that this
is the type of example

00:30:57.510 --> 00:30:59.040
lots of philosophers are talking about

00:30:59.040 --> 00:31:02.010
and people are making claims
about causation and complexity

00:31:02.010 --> 00:31:03.990
and as far as I know, no
one has actually tried

00:31:03.990 --> 00:31:06.360
to causally model it and
show this could be done.

00:31:06.360 --> 00:31:08.068
So as with other parts of the talk,

00:31:08.068 --> 00:31:10.110
I just think there's a
lot of opportunity here

00:31:10.110 --> 00:31:14.100
to take a lot of what
philosophers are doing informally

00:31:14.100 --> 00:31:15.300
and to think of it more rigorously.

00:31:15.300 --> 00:31:17.250
And I'll just say that through the process

00:31:17.250 --> 00:31:20.041
of building this model, I
learned a lot about the system

00:31:20.041 --> 00:31:23.850
that I had not gotten from
the philosophical treatments.

00:31:23.850 --> 00:31:27.333
So I do think it's illuminating.

00:31:28.410 --> 00:31:30.150
Okay, now in the last implication

00:31:30.150 --> 00:31:32.040
I'm gonna talk about stability.

00:31:32.040 --> 00:31:34.316
'Cause once again a
feature of these models

00:31:34.316 --> 00:31:36.570
is that looking at the feedback loops

00:31:36.570 --> 00:31:38.270
and the dynamic model can allow us

00:31:39.160 --> 00:31:41.670
to see the stability in sharing mechanisms

00:31:41.670 --> 00:31:44.245
underlying some more stable behavior.

00:31:44.245 --> 00:31:46.630
And I think stability
is one of those things

00:31:47.641 --> 00:31:51.510
that really has not been
talked about enough,

00:31:51.510 --> 00:31:54.570
at least not in this way in
the context of causation.

00:31:54.570 --> 00:31:57.930
And I think this is gonna be a payoff

00:31:57.930 --> 00:31:59.760
that also has lots of
practical implications.

00:31:59.760 --> 00:32:00.993
So for one thing,

00:32:01.980 --> 00:32:03.990
lots of inter interventions we care about,

00:32:03.990 --> 00:32:05.670
they're not just trying
to change the value

00:32:05.670 --> 00:32:07.828
of a single variable, but
really trying to get a system

00:32:07.828 --> 00:32:10.530
to be in a new stable state.

00:32:10.530 --> 00:32:11.910
So one example of this,

00:32:11.910 --> 00:32:13.478
at least if I'm understanding it,

00:32:13.478 --> 00:32:14.850
that would be chemotherapy.

00:32:14.850 --> 00:32:17.283
So not knowing the underlying science,

00:32:18.228 --> 00:32:19.290
but I take it what you're trying to do

00:32:19.290 --> 00:32:22.620
is you start with a
case where the body is,

00:32:22.620 --> 00:32:23.970
where the immune system is not such

00:32:23.970 --> 00:32:26.730
that it is able to eradicate the cancer

00:32:26.730 --> 00:32:28.980
and you want to apply chemotherapy

00:32:28.980 --> 00:32:30.780
to weaken the cancer to the point

00:32:30.780 --> 00:32:32.970
that then the immune system can take over.

00:32:32.970 --> 00:32:34.850
So what you're trying to
do is change the dynamics

00:32:34.850 --> 00:32:37.836
of the system so that once
you remove the therapy

00:32:37.836 --> 00:32:40.950
it will be, it'll have
a different stable state

00:32:40.950 --> 00:32:44.580
and one that is hopefully
more favorable to the patient.

00:32:44.580 --> 00:32:47.010
I also put in therapy therapy here,

00:32:47.010 --> 00:32:49.320
at least some types of talk
therapy are also trying

00:32:49.320 --> 00:32:51.630
to change your patterns of behavior

00:32:51.630 --> 00:32:53.730
so that you end up in a
different stable state

00:32:53.730 --> 00:32:56.823
as opposed to the ones that
make you more miserable.

00:32:59.040 --> 00:33:01.710
So I think to just
characterize what the goals are

00:33:01.710 --> 00:33:04.546
of many interventions, we do
need to talk about stability

00:33:04.546 --> 00:33:07.541
and that many policies shouldn't
be considered successful

00:33:07.541 --> 00:33:10.920
unless they actually
produce stable solutions

00:33:10.920 --> 00:33:13.320
and not just bring about an effect.

00:33:13.320 --> 00:33:15.870
And I think discussion
where this would really help

00:33:15.870 --> 00:33:18.360
is the discussion of causal extrapolation.

00:33:18.360 --> 00:33:19.500
So this is the question

00:33:19.500 --> 00:33:24.500
of how to infer causal
relations across populations.

00:33:24.690 --> 00:33:27.615
You find out that a
certain education policy

00:33:27.615 --> 00:33:30.450
worked in one place,
will it work in another?

00:33:30.450 --> 00:33:31.773
Very often it doesn't.

00:33:33.480 --> 00:33:35.880
And many people, including Nancy Carwright

00:33:35.880 --> 00:33:37.290
and Jeremy Hardie have talked

00:33:37.290 --> 00:33:40.020
about these types of inferences

00:33:40.020 --> 00:33:42.960
and the type of treatment they, and again,

00:33:42.960 --> 00:33:44.760
it's not just them,
everyone pretty much gives,

00:33:44.760 --> 00:33:48.060
are fairly static representations.

00:33:48.060 --> 00:33:50.010
So they talk about background factors.

00:33:50.010 --> 00:33:53.070
Here they have in mind
Mackie's INUS conditions

00:33:53.070 --> 00:33:54.060
where the idea is

00:33:54.060 --> 00:33:55.980
well, you have these
different populations,

00:33:55.980 --> 00:33:57.690
if it's a say a teaching context,

00:33:57.690 --> 00:34:00.990
maybe one population has a
better supply of teachers,

00:34:00.990 --> 00:34:02.540
the second population does not.

00:34:03.430 --> 00:34:06.120
So that's why the cause didn't generalize

00:34:06.120 --> 00:34:07.200
across the populations

00:34:07.200 --> 00:34:10.290
'cause one of them had the
necessary background factors

00:34:10.290 --> 00:34:11.853
while the second one did not.

00:34:13.576 --> 00:34:14.520
And what I wanna suggest

00:34:14.520 --> 00:34:17.340
is that even when we're
considering single population,

00:34:17.340 --> 00:34:19.350
so before you get to extrapolation,

00:34:19.350 --> 00:34:21.634
does this cause generalize?

00:34:21.634 --> 00:34:25.950
We shouldn't just think about
some static set of factors.

00:34:25.950 --> 00:34:28.650
If you're designing an educational policy

00:34:28.650 --> 00:34:31.800
and you're hoping that the
kids will do the homework,

00:34:31.800 --> 00:34:33.663
that would be helpful for the policy.

00:34:34.632 --> 00:34:36.090
But it should be the
case that if you discover

00:34:36.090 --> 00:34:39.090
that in a particular area kids
are not doing the homework,

00:34:39.090 --> 00:34:40.410
that there should be some mechanism

00:34:40.410 --> 00:34:41.940
that detects that this is happening

00:34:41.940 --> 00:34:43.680
and implements a feedback loop

00:34:43.680 --> 00:34:48.063
to shift the policy to
correct for that deficiency.

00:34:49.150 --> 00:34:50.400
So to think about a policy succeeding,

00:34:50.400 --> 00:34:52.800
we want not just to happen to succeed,

00:34:52.800 --> 00:34:54.125
but also to be dynamically stable

00:34:54.125 --> 00:34:56.970
in light of possible perturbations.

00:34:56.970 --> 00:34:58.950
And I think if we started modeling that,

00:34:58.950 --> 00:35:01.860
which I think requires dynamic models,

00:35:01.860 --> 00:35:03.270
that would also help understanding

00:35:03.270 --> 00:35:06.780
how we are not just lucky
when we get generalizability.

00:35:06.780 --> 00:35:09.570
Okay, so just to
conclude, so first of all,

00:35:09.570 --> 00:35:11.160
I've argued that many basic assumptions

00:35:11.160 --> 00:35:13.590
of causal models are timescale relative.

00:35:13.590 --> 00:35:16.708
I think timescale relativity
is hugely important

00:35:16.708 --> 00:35:19.052
and presents both
philosophical opportunities

00:35:19.052 --> 00:35:21.097
for understanding the
conditions of attribution

00:35:21.097 --> 00:35:25.650
of causal models and for also
doing better causal inference

00:35:25.650 --> 00:35:27.570
and policy evaluation.

00:35:27.570 --> 00:35:30.803
And also that the notion
of dynamical stability

00:35:30.803 --> 00:35:33.060
is different than the notions of stability

00:35:33.060 --> 00:35:34.740
that exist in literature

00:35:34.740 --> 00:35:37.620
because most of the existing discussions

00:35:37.620 --> 00:35:40.500
are implicitly considering static models.

00:35:40.500 --> 00:35:42.036
So thank you very much.

00:35:42.036 --> 00:35:45.036
(audience clapping)

00:35:46.380 --> 00:35:47.380
- Questions.

00:35:50.852 --> 00:35:54.432
- [Audience Member] Hi,
thanks for the great talk.

00:35:54.432 --> 00:35:57.430
So when you do these model transformations

00:35:58.774 --> 00:36:00.480
going from a lower level
causal model through,

00:36:00.480 --> 00:36:05.427
I think you put it exogenization
and I forget the other one.

00:36:05.427 --> 00:36:06.618
- A equilibration.

00:36:06.618 --> 00:36:09.210
- [Audience Member] Yeah.

00:36:09.210 --> 00:36:11.640
Is there a change in
the kinds of questions

00:36:11.640 --> 00:36:13.350
that you can ask, the
kind of inference queries

00:36:13.350 --> 00:36:17.490
you can pose against the abstracted model,

00:36:17.490 --> 00:36:19.380
and is there a change in the accuracy

00:36:19.380 --> 00:36:21.360
of the inferences, right?

00:36:21.360 --> 00:36:22.950
If you're making these assumptions about,

00:36:22.950 --> 00:36:24.930
you know, this being a steady state,

00:36:24.930 --> 00:36:27.570
it seems like there might
be some loss in accuracy

00:36:27.570 --> 00:36:28.935
and so is there a precise way

00:36:28.935 --> 00:36:31.590
in which you can know what that will be?

00:36:31.590 --> 00:36:33.165
- Yeah, that's a great question

00:36:33.165 --> 00:36:37.973
and the answer's
ultimately yes there can be

00:36:37.973 --> 00:36:40.055
a loss in accuracy, but
I'll have to be precise

00:36:40.055 --> 00:36:41.010
about what that is.

00:36:41.010 --> 00:36:43.590
So I do think, and it's been
important for me to argue

00:36:43.590 --> 00:36:47.310
that the models are
correct in the domains of,

00:36:47.310 --> 00:36:49.590
or at the scales of
interest and to at least,

00:36:49.590 --> 00:36:51.720
so in my Intervening in Letting Go paper,

00:36:51.720 --> 00:36:55.620
I explain yeah, because you
actually do need to revise

00:36:55.620 --> 00:36:57.810
some adequacy conditions
and causal models.

00:36:57.810 --> 00:37:00.500
But I at least I've
argued that could be done.

00:37:00.500 --> 00:37:01.950
But there is a loss,

00:37:01.950 --> 00:37:06.240
and the loss is basically
that if you have a system

00:37:06.240 --> 00:37:09.960
that intervening on certain
variables will destabilize it,

00:37:09.960 --> 00:37:11.820
you're not always gonna
be able to see that

00:37:11.820 --> 00:37:14.790
at the you know, larger timescale.

00:37:14.790 --> 00:37:16.290
There's just, so the
dynamic model will kind of,

00:37:16.290 --> 00:37:17.250
you'll see the feedback loop,

00:37:17.250 --> 00:37:19.157
you'll know the result of intervening

00:37:19.157 --> 00:37:22.680
while other models you know,

00:37:22.680 --> 00:37:25.080
because you're just not zooming in enough,

00:37:25.080 --> 00:37:25.950
you won't be able to see that.

00:37:25.950 --> 00:37:27.243
So that that is a loss.

00:37:28.663 --> 00:37:32.137
And yeah, and I think
it's an argument for,

00:37:33.270 --> 00:37:34.950
you know, doing more
work and dynamic modeling

00:37:34.950 --> 00:37:36.675
and exploring it.

00:37:36.675 --> 00:37:38.850
But again, part of why I do wanna argue

00:37:38.850 --> 00:37:40.230
is even if that's helpful to do,

00:37:40.230 --> 00:37:42.270
we can still understand
models in the own terms.

00:37:42.270 --> 00:37:43.140
'Cause at the end of the day,

00:37:43.140 --> 00:37:45.240
I think we're always at a timescale.

00:37:45.240 --> 00:37:47.748
So we're never gonna
get to the bottom level.

00:37:47.748 --> 00:37:50.370
So we better have a story about why,

00:37:50.370 --> 00:37:54.547
you know, why at least we
can understand models as,

00:37:54.547 --> 00:37:56.850
you know, as being potentially legitimate

00:37:56.850 --> 00:37:58.467
at different timescales.

00:37:58.467 --> 00:38:01.217
Even if like any model they
might break down sometimes.

00:38:05.670 --> 00:38:06.840
- Thanks so much.

00:38:06.840 --> 00:38:10.080
So there is something that
a lot of what you said

00:38:10.080 --> 00:38:13.380
just resonates with thinking
about practice in physics

00:38:13.380 --> 00:38:16.080
where you wanna understand
stability under perturbations

00:38:16.080 --> 00:38:17.340
for any system you're looking at

00:38:17.340 --> 00:38:19.830
and you want to try to look
at different timescales

00:38:19.830 --> 00:38:21.420
to see if there's you know,

00:38:21.420 --> 00:38:22.800
things that you have to take into account

00:38:22.800 --> 00:38:26.760
if you're really trying to
extrapolate over large scales.

00:38:26.760 --> 00:38:31.038
And I was curious how you'd
characterize the contrast

00:38:31.038 --> 00:38:35.340
between the way that
dynamical causal modeling

00:38:35.340 --> 00:38:38.452
approaches those that distinguishes it

00:38:38.452 --> 00:38:39.870
from just the sort of standard techniques

00:38:39.870 --> 00:38:41.610
if you just had a partial
differential equation

00:38:41.610 --> 00:38:44.490
and you're looking at stability
and perturbation analysis.

00:38:44.490 --> 00:38:46.560
- Awesome, thanks so
much for your question.

00:38:46.560 --> 00:38:47.400
And one thing just to say,

00:38:47.400 --> 00:38:50.070
so I think the one person I've seen,

00:38:50.070 --> 00:38:50.903
and maybe there's a bunch,

00:38:50.903 --> 00:38:53.370
but the one I've come across
who I think really does

00:38:53.370 --> 00:38:55.380
get the stability right
in the context of physics

00:38:55.380 --> 00:38:59.850
is Yemima Ben-Menahem in her
Causality and Physics book

00:38:59.850 --> 00:39:02.070
and she also has some interesting things

00:39:02.070 --> 00:39:04.841
to say about stability and
historical explanation.

00:39:04.841 --> 00:39:06.291
So I'll throw that out there.

00:39:07.830 --> 00:39:09.780
In some ways they might
not be all that different

00:39:09.780 --> 00:39:12.180
or at least here's one thing
that can happen when you,

00:39:12.180 --> 00:39:15.557
when you find yourself modeling
something like the governor

00:39:15.557 --> 00:39:17.370
and so something like equilibration,

00:39:17.370 --> 00:39:18.750
it's not radically different

00:39:18.750 --> 00:39:20.940
than what you do when you have a set

00:39:20.940 --> 00:39:23.340
of differential equations
and you, you know,

00:39:23.340 --> 00:39:25.173
solve by sending something to zero.

00:39:27.150 --> 00:39:29.550
And in that sense, on the positive side

00:39:29.550 --> 00:39:31.140
it creates a continuity

00:39:31.140 --> 00:39:35.370
between differential equation
practice and causal modeling.

00:39:35.370 --> 00:39:37.650
On the other hand, you might
be inclined to say like look,

00:39:37.650 --> 00:39:39.750
everything you were doing
I could have just done

00:39:39.750 --> 00:39:42.120
with the differential equations

00:39:42.120 --> 00:39:46.680
and it's a discussion we,
you know, need to have,

00:39:46.680 --> 00:39:48.030
whether that's in fact true.

00:39:48.030 --> 00:39:51.780
I mean for me the
continuity is a good thing

00:39:51.780 --> 00:39:56.780
and I guess I'd say at least this

00:39:57.240 --> 00:39:58.650
'cause it's a longer discussion,

00:39:58.650 --> 00:39:59.520
which is, so first of all,

00:39:59.520 --> 00:40:03.180
I don't think that my
discussion says anything general

00:40:03.180 --> 00:40:06.570
about whether dynamic systems
in general can be modeled.

00:40:06.570 --> 00:40:07.800
What I've shown at best is that

00:40:07.800 --> 00:40:09.930
when you have dynamical
systems that can be written

00:40:09.930 --> 00:40:12.030
in a certain canonical form,

00:40:12.030 --> 00:40:14.130
they can also be interpreted causally.

00:40:14.130 --> 00:40:15.210
Now whether that's,

00:40:15.210 --> 00:40:17.010
in some cases that might
not contribute that much

00:40:17.010 --> 00:40:18.120
to the modeling practice,

00:40:18.120 --> 00:40:19.650
but philosophically I
do think it's important

00:40:19.650 --> 00:40:22.980
because you see so many
claims about you know,

00:40:22.980 --> 00:40:24.690
again, not being able to have causation

00:40:24.690 --> 00:40:26.880
and dynamical systems and here we see

00:40:26.880 --> 00:40:29.610
that the analysis is similar.

00:40:29.610 --> 00:40:33.150
Now in terms of what we learn,

00:40:33.150 --> 00:40:35.070
I think it creates a bridge
where you can argue either way.

00:40:35.070 --> 00:40:37.980
You can argue that
fundamentally the assumptions

00:40:37.980 --> 00:40:39.330
in the differential equations model

00:40:39.330 --> 00:40:40.770
they thought of were
non causal assumptions

00:40:40.770 --> 00:40:42.390
are causal assumptions.

00:40:42.390 --> 00:40:44.340
Or you can go in the
other way and say look,

00:40:44.340 --> 00:40:47.565
you know, the causal is
just a unhelpful abstraction

00:40:47.565 --> 00:40:49.320
from the differential equation.

00:40:49.320 --> 00:40:52.064
So you probably know
which way I want to go,

00:40:52.064 --> 00:40:55.080
but yeah, that's a longer discussion.

00:40:55.080 --> 00:40:58.755
- So Chris, we have a Ben-Menahem
was part of a discussion,

00:40:58.755 --> 00:41:02.223
so the video is available
on the website if you go.

00:41:03.390 --> 00:41:05.070
So I have a very quick question

00:41:05.070 --> 00:41:08.970
before the next speaker comes up to set up

00:41:08.970 --> 00:41:13.500
and that is we can treat these
exogenous factors, right.

00:41:13.500 --> 00:41:17.040
So in the rainfall wheat
example that you showed,

00:41:17.040 --> 00:41:17.940
because they operate

00:41:17.940 --> 00:41:20.190
on completely different timescales, right?

00:41:20.190 --> 00:41:23.340
So the sort of a decoupling of timescales,

00:41:23.340 --> 00:41:25.380
but underlying that,

00:41:25.380 --> 00:41:27.720
isn't there an additional
assumption as well

00:41:27.720 --> 00:41:31.680
that the variations on
that other timescale

00:41:31.680 --> 00:41:34.743
that imposes on the
system are really small?

00:41:37.140 --> 00:41:39.270
When we are studying on the timescales

00:41:39.270 --> 00:41:41.370
that we have chosen to study,

00:41:41.370 --> 00:41:45.570
the variations on, not only
is the timescale longer,

00:41:45.570 --> 00:41:49.020
but the variations that it
can produce in the system

00:41:49.020 --> 00:41:52.170
are very small over the interval of study

00:41:52.170 --> 00:41:54.090
because we are choosing to focus

00:41:54.090 --> 00:41:55.830
on a particular timescale, right?

00:41:55.830 --> 00:41:56.760
- That sounds right.

00:41:56.760 --> 00:41:57.630
- Yeah, okay.

00:41:57.630 --> 00:41:58.463
Yeah.

00:41:58.463 --> 00:42:00.169
So that's an additional
assumption that it doesn't have.

00:42:00.169 --> 00:42:01.002
- Yeah.

00:42:01.002 --> 00:42:01.835
- Okay.

00:42:01.835 --> 00:42:03.180
- Yeah.

00:42:03.180 --> 00:42:04.013
- Great.

00:42:04.013 --> 00:42:04.846
Thank you so much.

00:42:04.846 --> 00:42:06.716
Let's move on to our next speaker.

